              is like the restriction to the “complement of all formal disks”. Finally
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{\mathbb{Q}}
              may be understood as the restriction to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ran+space"} Ran space
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(\mathbb{Z})
              (
              %a{:href => "#Gaitsgory11"}> Gaitsgory 11
              ), roughly the colimit of the restriction of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to the complement of finitely many points, as this set of points ranges through all points.
          %p
            In view of remark
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TwoThirdHexagon"}
            we may regard the following fact as an refinement of the traditional arithmetic fracture theorem.
          #CompletionTorsionAdjointModalityForModuleSpectra.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_5 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
